{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789298493,"nonce":"6Y/59LHV+skDoZ14Bp+LrINSYJQ=","message":"Ash side, redirected by the owner (2026-09-13): no native library and no\nresolver by name. Ash takes module registrations ahead of running the\ncode, so a Wren class enters the decoded program directly: an HOBJ type\nin the type table plus entries in the shared findex numbering, each a\ntyped native whose C target the host supplies (or, later, an AIR body\nthe host hands in, so the tiers can inline). A Haxe call into it is then\nan ordinary call in ash's own IR at every tier. Filed on ash as\n05cedba's replacement (registration entry, high priority).\n\nCompile-time half, confirmed against the HL target: `extern class` is\nrefused (\"Extern type not supported\"); the build macro emits an ordinary\nclass with @:hlNative(\"caribou\", \"\u003csymbol\u003e\") methods and an\nhl.Abstract\u003c\"caribou_obj\"\u003e handle field, marked @:keep. The bytecode\nthen declares the natives by (lib, name), which registration satisfies.\n\nOrder: ash registration entry (ash side) -\u003e caribou-ash registers\npublished Wren classes through it -\u003e build macro. Meanwhile the\ncaribou-side work that does not depend on the ash entry can proceed:\npublishing Wren classes into the registry from wren_lift's class layouts\nand method tables, and the Haxe-holds-Wren proxy (0268f40's other half).","files":null}]}